TWO NEW ENV KNOBS, both CLASS-FREE by construction: KICAD_FANOUT_AUTO_DOGBONE
(#669, default OFF) and KICAD_INRUN_FLOOR_SYNC (#650, default ON). Both are
read via env_knobs INSIDE shared engine functions -- the fanout core and
batch_route respectively -- so both fronts observe them identically from the
environment. No dialog control, no FLAG_PARAMS row, nothing to mirror. This is
the same status as the existing ORACLE_UNION / LEGACY_ORACLE knobs.

#688 IS THE INVERTED CASE AGAIN (like d4831b93 last time): a GUI bug fixed in
a SHARED module. The routing WORKER thread called pcbnew.SaveBoard -- a
wx-backed C++ call -- while the UI thread sat in ShowModal, deadlocking the
plugin on Windows (py-spy named both sides). The guard lives ENGINE-side in
py_router/ui_thread.py because the engine makes such calls of its own (the
live-fill provider in kicad_parser, reached from exact_unconnected and
plane_fragility every oracle round), so it could not live only in
kicad_routing_plugin/. wx is imported lazily; the CLI has no wx and degrades
to a direct call. gui_utils.save_board_via_ui_thread is a thin alias, so there
is ONE implementation. Parity direction: CLI-was-fine, GUI-was-broken,
shared-module-fix.

#693 IS GUI-ONLY BY NATURE and is the one range item that WAS a real gap:
"Fix DRC settings after routing" gated apply_targets_to_board but NOT
update_live_drc_floors, at ALL FIVE call sites (signal/planes/diff/fanout tabs
+ the plan executor), so an unchecked box still rewrote the board's Board
Setup floors. The CLI has always gated its twin (fix_project_for_output) on
--no-fix-drc-settings, so this was the GUI diverging FROM the CLI, and the fix
restores parity rather than creating it. Two sites needed more than a gate:
fanout's get_shared_params() never carried the flag at all, and ai_plan.py had
no notion of it -- on both, a bare .get('fix_drc_settings', True) would have
been a silent no-op. Now covered by a dedicated gate (below).

#650's in-run floor sync lives in batch_route (shared), so both fronts get it
free -- Class-1 shape. Its two call sites are inside the engine function, not
in a CLI main(), which is why test_cli_postpass_coverage still reads
8/15/1/0/0/0 rather than flagging a new Class-2 post-pass.

